QB.add(24,[{"id":24001,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Where does meiosis occur and what is its key outcome?","o":["In the germline; gametes have half the parent’s chromosomes","In somatic cells throughout the body; daughter cells have half the chromosomes","In the germline; gametes have the same chromosome number as the parent","In somatic cells; daughter cells are identical and diploid","In mitochondria; DNA is copied"],"e":"Meiosis happens in the <b>germline</b> (cells making sperm and eggs). Its point is that <b>daughter cells have half the chromosome number</b>. One DNA replication then <b>two divisions</b> → four haploid gametes. Mitosis keeps the same number.","w":["Germline.","Half.","That is mitosis.","Not."],"k":"Meiosis halves chromosome number.","rn":[["","Meiosis: number of chromosomes in the daughter cells?","Meiosis: number of chromosomes in the daughter cells?","Half of the parent cell"],["","Meiosis","Meiosis","Division making gametes; Daughters have half chromosomes"]]},{"id":24002,"t":"Meiosis","s":"What is the chromosome make-up of a human somatic cell vs a gamete?","o":["Somatic: 44 + XX/XY (2n = 46); gamete: 22 + X or Y (n = 23)","Somatic: 22 + X or Y (n = 23); gamete: 44 + XX/XY (2n = 46)","Somatic: 44 + XX/XY (2n = 46); gamete: 44 + X or Y (n = 45)","Somatic: 46 + XX/XY (2n = 48); gamete: 23 + X or Y (n = 24)","Both: 44 + XX/XY (46)"],"e":"Somatic cells: <b>diploid (2n)</b>, <b>46</b> = 44 autosomes + XX or XY (written 44XX/44XY). Gametes: <b>haploid (n)</b>, <b>23</b> = 22 autosomes + X or Y (22X/22Y).","w":["Swapped.","Halved.","Not.","Not."],"k":"46 vs 23.","rn":[["","Somatic cell vs gamete: number of chromosomes","Somatic cell vs gamete: number of chromosomes","Somatic: 46; Gamete: 23"],["","What are the 46 chromosomes of a somatic cell made of?","What are the 46 chromosomes of a somatic cell made of?","44 autosomes; + XX or XY"]]},{"id":24003,"t":"Meiosis","s":"What are homologues?","o":["Maternal and paternal copies of the same chromosome","Two identical sister chromatids of one chromosome","The two sex chromosomes, X and Y","Mitochondrial chromosomes","Chromosomes from unrelated people"],"e":"46 chromosomes: 23 maternal, 23 paternal; these are <b>homologues</b>. Each parent contributes one gamete.","w":["Sisters.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Homologues = maternal + paternal copies.","rn":[["","What are the maternal and paternal copies of the same chromosome called?","What are the maternal and paternal copies of the same chromosome called?","Homologues"]]},{"id":24004,"t":"Meiosis","f":"di06","s":"In the diplontic life cycle, which is the only haploid stage?","o":["The gametes","The zygote","The mature organism","Somatic cells","The embryo"],"e":"In the diplontic life cycle the organism is diploid and the <b>gametes are the only haploid stage</b>: meiosis → gametes (n) → fertilisation → zygote (2n) → mature organism (2n).","w":["2n.","2n.","2n.","2n."],"k":"Gametes only haploid stage.","rn":[["","Diplontic life cycle: first 2 stages?","Diplontic life cycle: first 2 stages?","Mature organism (2n); Meiosis to gametes (n)"],["","In the life cycle, which process turns haploid gametes into a diploid zygote?","In the life cycle, which process turns haploid gametes into a diploid zygote?","Fertilisation"]]},{"id":24005,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Why does sexual reproduction produce progeny dissimilar to their parents?","o":["Each parent gives a gamete, and recombination mixes their DNA","Each parent gives a diploid cell, and mitosis mixes their DNA","Mutations always occur in every gamete","Offspring receive only maternal DNA","Mitosis changes DNA in the zygote"],"e":"Sexual reproduction requires <b>gametes</b> from meiosis; each parent contributes one, and <b>recombination</b> during meiosis mixes material.","w":["Haploid; meiosis.","Not always.","Both.","Not."],"k":"Gametes + recombination → variation.","rn":[["","What if there were no recombination?","What if there were no recombination?","Offspring like parents; No genetic variation"],["","Are the chromosomes in gametes 100% the same as the parent's?","Are the chromosomes in gametes 100% the same as the parent's?","No, slightly different; Recombined"]]},{"id":24006,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which statement about DNA replication in meiosis is correct?","o":["Once, before meiosis I only","Before both meiosis I and meiosis II","Only before meiosis II","Not at all during meiosis","Three times"],"e":"Meiosis I comes after DNA replication; <b>no replication before meiosis II</b>. Prophase/metaphase/anaphase/telophase are the same names as mitosis; microtubules form the spindle.","w":["Once.","I.","Once.","Not."],"k":"One replication, two divisions.","rn":[["","What 3 things does meiosis I involve?","What 3 things does meiosis I involve?","DNA replication; Recombination; Cell division"],["","Does DNA replication still happen in meiosis?","Does DNA replication still happen in meiosis?","Yes, before meiosis I"],["","What are the 2 divisions of meiosis called?","What are the 2 divisions of meiosis called?","Meiosis I; Meiosis II"]]},{"id":24007,"t":"Meiosis","f":"di08","s":"Which events occur in prophase I?","o":["Chromosomes condense, pair as tetrads and recombine at chiasmata","Chromosomes condense and sister chromatids separate to the poles","Chromosomes decondense and nuclear membranes re-form","DNA replicates ready for division","Cytokinesis divides the cell"],"e":"<b>Prophase I</b>: chromosomes <b>condense and pair</b> with their homologue; each pair of sister chromatids aligns as a <b>tetrad</b>; <b>recombination</b> occurs (crossing over at the chiasma).","w":["Anaphase II.","Telophase.","Before.","Telophase."],"k":"Prophase I: pairing + recombination.","rn":[["","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","Condense; Pair up with their homologue"],["","Tetrad","Tetrad","Homologous chromosomes lined up; Each a sister chromatid pair"]]},{"id":24008,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Name the crossover point between homologous chromatids during prophase I.","e":"Chiasmata form where homologous chromatids recombine in prophase I, creating variability.","k":"Chiasma.","a":["chiasma","chiasmata","crossover","crossing over"],"rn":[["","Chiasma","Chiasma","Point of crossover; Between chromosomes"]]},{"id":24009,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Is recombination a mistake? Why does it matter?","o":["No – it is normal and creates variability","Yes – it is a copying error, though it is usually repaired before division","Yes – it is always an error","It only happens in mitosis","It reduces variation"],"e":"Recombination is <b>not a mistake</b>; it happens regularly in every meiosis and <b>underpins genetic variability</b> – otherwise little variation as a species.","w":["It happens in every meiosis and is not an error.","Not error.","Meiosis.","Increases."],"k":"Recombination drives variation."},{"id":24010,"t":"Meiosis","s":"After recombination, how do the chromosomes in a gamete compare to the parent's original chromosomes?","o":["Mixed – they carry segments from the other parent","Identical to the father’s or mother’s original chromosomes, as recombination only reorders","Identical to the mother’s original chromosomes","Completely new random DNA","Missing half their genes"],"e":"Each gamete carries chromosome 1 from one side, but <b>not 100% the same</b>: swapped segments from the other parent.","w":["Recombination exchanges segments, so they are mixed.","Mixed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Recombinant chromosomes."},{"id":24011,"t":"Meiosis","s":"In which phase does meiotic recombination take place?","o":["Prophase I","Metaphase I","Anaphase II","Telophase II","S phase"],"e":"The lecturer placed it at metaphase I in the recording, but the <b>speaker notes and figure say prophase I</b>. Go with prophase I.","w":["Recording slip.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Recombination in prophase I.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of meiosis I after prophase I?","What are the 3 stages of meiosis I after prophase I?","Metaphase I; Anaphase I; Telophase I"],["","What are the 3 stages of prophase I?","What are the 3 stages of prophase I?","Early prophase I; Mid-prophase I; Late prophase I to prometaphase"],["","Meiosis II: first 2 stages?","Meiosis II: first 2 stages?","Prophase II; Metaphase II"]]},{"id":24012,"t":"Meiosis","f":"di10","s":"What separates in anaphase I compared with anaphase II?","o":["Anaphase I: homologues separate; anaphase II: sister chromatids separate","Anaphase I: sister chromatids separate; anaphase II: homologues separate","Both anaphases separate sister chromatids","Both anaphases separate homologues","Neither separates anything"],"e":"Anaphase I: <b>tetrad separated</b>, sister chromatids <b>remain together</b>. Anaphase II: <b>sister chromatids separated</b>. Memory aid: meiosis I separates the Pairs, meiosis II the sisTers.","w":["Swapped.","I homologues.","II sisters.","They do."],"k":"I: homologues; II: sisters.","rn":[["","Anaphase I vs anaphase II: what separates?","Anaphase I vs anaphase II: what separates?","I: tetrad (homologues); II: sister chromatids"],["","In which division are sister chromatids separated?","In which division are sister chromatids separated?","Meiosis II (anaphase II)"],["","What happens in anaphase II?","What happens in anaphase II?","Sister chromatids are separated"]]},{"id":24013,"t":"Meiosis","f":"di12","s":"What is the final product of meiosis?","o":["Four haploid cells, each a different recombined mix","Four haploid cells, each genetically identical","Two diploid cells, each a different mix","Four diploid cells","One haploid cell"],"e":"Telophase II: nuclear membrane forms, chromosomes decondense, cytokinesis → <b>four haploid gametes</b>.","w":["Different.","Haploid, four.","Haploid.","Four."],"k":"Meiosis → 4 haploid gametes.","rn":[["","Image card: Which stage of meiosis is this micrograph?","Which stage of meiosis is this micrograph?","Products (4 haploid cells)"],["","Are the chromosomes in gametes 100% the same as the parent's?","Are the chromosomes in gametes 100% the same as the parent's?","No, slightly different; Recombined"]]},{"id":24015,"t":"Meiotic Errors","f":"di15","s":"Unequal crossing over between two chromosomes produces one with genes A, C and another with A, B, B, C. What has happened?","o":["Gene B was lost from one and duplicated in the other","Gene B was inverted on both chromosomes and then translocated to a new site","Normal recombination, with no change","Non-disjunction of the chromosomes","A point mutation in gene B"],"e":"Recombination errors: unequal exchange → <b>gene loss or duplication</b>; broken segments can also cause <b>inversions and translocations</b>. One chromosome is “shortchanged”.","w":["Unequal exchange deletes from one and duplicates in the other.","Abnormal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Unequal crossing over → loss/duplication.","rn":[["","Image card: This recombination exchanged unequal amounts. What does each chromosome end up with?","This recombination exchanged unequal amounts. What does each chromosome end up with?","Top: lost B (gene loss); Bottom: B twice (gene duplication)"],["","Which gene is duplicated in CMT1A?","Which gene is duplicated in CMT1A?","PMP22"]]},{"id":24016,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease type 1A results from which mechanism?","o":["Unequal crossing over duplicating PMP22","Non-disjunction of chromosome 17 in meiosis I, giving trisomy 17 in the child","A mitochondrial DNA mutation affecting nerves","A point mutation in CFTR","Non-disjunction of X"],"e":"<b>CMT1A</b> (most common inherited peripheral neuropathy, 1 in 2,500): <b>unequal crossing over</b> between >98% identical <b>24 kb repeats on chromosome 17</b> → <b>1.5 Mbp duplication</b> of <b>PMP22</b>. Deletion of PMP22 also causes neuropathy. (Context only.)","w":["It is unequal crossing over at chromosome 17 repeats.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CMT1A = PMP22 duplication.","rn":[["","Which gene is duplicated in CMT1A?","Which gene is duplicated in CMT1A?","PMP22"],["","What causes CMT1A?","What causes CMT1A?","Unequal crossing over; Between repeats, chromosome 17"]]},{"id":24017,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Why is recombination “quite dangerous” despite being normal?","o":["It breaks DNA; segments may be lost","It always causes cancer in the cell where the exchange has taken place","It prevents meiosis from completing","It doubles chromosome number","It only occurs with radiation"],"e":"Recombination involves <b>breaking the DNA</b>; a part cut off may be <b>lost</b> or <b>stick onto another chromosome</b> → translocations and inversions. Not error-proof.","w":["Not always; the danger is broken DNA and lost/translocated segments.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"DNA breaks risk translocation.","rn":[["","Why is recombination dangerous?","Why is recombination dangerous?","Involves breaking DNA; Cut part lost; Or sticks elsewhere"]]},{"id":24018,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Chromosomes fail to separate during division, giving gametes with extra or missing chromosomes. Name this.","e":"Non-disjunction in meiosis I makes all four gametes abnormal; in meiosis II, two of four.","k":"Non-disjunction.","a":["non-disjunction","nondisjunction","non disjunction"],"rn":[["","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","2 with an extra chromosome; 2 missing a chromosome"],["","Non-disjunction","Non-disjunction","Separation errors; Of chromosomes in meiosis"],["","Non-disjunction in meiosis II: what gametes result?","Non-disjunction in meiosis II: what gametes result?","2 normal; 1 with an extra chromosome; 1 missing a chromosome"]]},{"id":24019,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Non-disjunction occurs in meiosis I. What proportion of the resulting four gametes are abnormal?","o":["All four","Two","One","Three","None"],"e":"In meiosis I non-disjunction, one cell gets both homologues and the other none, so <b>all four gametes</b> are abnormal. In meiosis II non-disjunction, only the gametes from the affected cell are abnormal.","w":["Meiosis II.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Meiosis I ND → all abnormal.","rn":[["","Non-disjunction","Non-disjunction","Separation errors; Of chromosomes in meiosis"],["","In which divisions can non-disjunction happen?","In which divisions can non-disjunction happen?","Meiosis I; Meiosis II"],["","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","2 with an extra chromosome; 2 missing a chromosome"]]},{"id":24020,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Non-disjunction in mitosis produces distinct cell lines in one person. Name this.","e":"Mitotic non-disjunction gives distinct cell lines (chimerism/mosaicism).","k":"Chimerism.","a":["chimerism","mosaicism","chimera","mosaic"],"rn":[["","What does non-disjunction in mitosis produce?","What does non-disjunction in mitosis produce?","Distinct cell lines; Chimerism"]]},{"id":24021,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which trisomies are compatible with life?","o":["Trisomy 13, 18, 21 and sex chromosome aneuploidies","Trisomy 1, 2, 3 and sex chromosome aneuploidies","Trisomy 13, 18, 21 only, not sex chromosomes","All trisomies","None"],"e":"Trisomy/monosomy is <b>usually fatal</b>, except chromosomes <b>13, 18, 21</b> or <b>sex chromosomes</b>. Others → embryonic lethality (appearing as miscarriage). ~50% of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities.","w":["Lethal.","Sex too.","Most lethal.","Some."],"k":"Viable: 13, 18, 21, sex chromosomes.","rn":[["","Which chromosomes are the exceptions where trisomy is not always fatal?","Which chromosomes are the exceptions where trisomy is not always fatal?","13; 18; 21; Sex chromosomes"],["","What can a sex chromosome be?","What can a sex chromosome be?","X or Y"]]},{"id":24022,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which pairing of trisomy and syndrome is correct?","o":["13 – Patau’s; 18 – Edward’s; 21 – Down","13 – Edward’s; 18 – Patau’s; 21 – Down","13 – Down; 18 – Edward’s; 21 – Patau’s","13 – Patau’s; 18 – Down; 21 – Edward’s","All Down"],"e":"Trisomy <b>21 = Down</b>, <b>13 = Patau's</b>, <b>18 = Edward's</b>. (The recording slipped; use the slide.) Memory aid: 13 Patau (P before E), 18 Edward's (E for Eighteen), 21 Down.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Swapped.","Not."],"k":"13 Patau, 18 Edward, 21 Down.","rn":[["","What are the 3 viable autosomal trisomies?","What are the 3 viable autosomal trisomies?","Trisomy 21 (Down); Trisomy 13 (Patau); Trisomy 18 (Edward)"],["","Down syndrome vs Patau/Edward: survival","Down syndrome vs Patau/Edward: survival","Down: viable; Patau/Edward: lethal early"]]},{"id":24023,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A fetus with trisomy 13 has intrauterine growth retardation and organ abnormalities. What is the typical outlook?","o":["Many die early; 5–10% survive a year","Most survive into adulthood with only mild problems after early surgery","Many die before birth; 50% survive a year","Normal life expectancy","Full recovery after surgery"],"e":"Patau's (trisomy 13): slow growth before birth, low birth weight, multi-organ abnormalities; many die before birth or within a month; <b>5–10% live past one year</b>. Key point: trisomies produce significant system-wide effects.","w":["Only 5–10% survive the first year.","5–10%.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Patau's outlook.","rn":[["","When do many with trisomy 13 or 18 die?","When do many with trisomy 13 or 18 die?","Before birth; Or within the first month"]]},{"id":24024,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"About what proportion of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities?","o":["About 50%","About 5%","About 25%","About 90%","About 1%"],"e":"<b>~50% of early miscarriages</b> are due to chromosomal abnormalities (you don't need the exact percentage).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"~50% early miscarriages.","rn":[["","What proportion of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities?","What proportion of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities?","~50%"]]},{"id":24025,"t":"Meiotic Errors","f":"di19","s":"How does incidence of chromosomal abnormality change with maternal age on the Hook (1981) graph?","o":["Flat until mid-30s, then rising steeply","Rising steadily from the teens onwards","Highest in teenagers, then falling","Constant at all ages","Peaking at 25"],"e":"Incidence stays flat until the mid-30s, then rises steeply; the lecturer: increases dramatically between 30 and 40. Key point is increasing incidence with maternal age.","w":["Flat.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Maternal age ↑ risk."},{"id":24026,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which statement about paternal age and chromosomal errors is correct?","o":["It matters too – male gametes can carry errors","It has no effect on chromosomal errors","Only paternal age matters, not maternal","Older fathers always have healthier children","Male gametes never have errors"],"e":"All are strongly linked to <b>maternal AND paternal age</b>; errors can affect male gametes too (Janeczko et al. 2020 chart).","w":["It does.","Both.","Not.","They can."],"k":"Paternal age matters too.","rn":[["","Can age-related errors affect male gametes?","Can age-related errors affect male gametes?","Yes"]]},{"id":24027,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Why is non-disjunction of sex chromosomes better tolerated than of autosomes?","o":["One X is inactivated, and Y has few genes","X and Y both carry very many genes","Sex chromosomes do not replicate","Sex chromosome errors are always repaired","They are mitochondrial"],"e":"Autosomes contain important functional genes. Sex-chromosome errors are tolerated because of <b>X chromosome inactivation</b> (extra X also inactivated) and the <b>Y has few genes</b>.","w":["Few genes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"X inactivation + small Y.","rn":[["","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Y has few genes"],["","X chromosome inactivation","X chromosome inactivation","One female X inactivated; Packed: genes inaccessible"]]},{"id":24028,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A short adolescent girl with underdeveloped ovaries and neck webbing has a 45,XO karyotype. Name the syndrome.","e":"Turner syndrome (XO) may be diagnosed only at puberty.","k":"Turner syndrome.","a":["turner syndrome","turner","turners syndrome","turner's syndrome","turner's"],"rn":[["","What is the karyotype in Turner Syndrome?","What is the karyotype in Turner Syndrome?","XO female"]]},{"id":24029,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A man investigated for infertility is found to be 47,XXY. Name the syndrome.","e":"Klinefelter syndrome (XXY); sex chromosome aneuploidies are tolerated because one X is inactivated and Y has few genes.","k":"Klinefelter syndrome.","a":["klinefelter syndrome","klinefelter","klinefelters","klinefelter's syndrome","klinefelter's"],"rn":[["","What is the karyotype in Klinefelter syndrome?","What is the karyotype in Klinefelter syndrome?","XXY male"]]},{"id":24030,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Why are XXX females and XYY males often not identified?","o":["Extra X is inactivated and Y has few genes, so few symptoms","Extra X and Y genes are all active, so symptoms are mild","They are always lethal","They are always diagnosed at birth","They have Down syndrome"],"e":"XXX females and XYY males are often <b>not identified</b> from signs or symptoms.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"XXX, XYY often unrecognised.","rn":[["","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Y has few genes"],["","Why is XXX tolerated?","Why is XXX tolerated?","The extra X is inactivated"]]},{"id":24031,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"What is the purpose of X chromosome inactivation in females?","o":["To avoid double X gene products","To destroy one X permanently, removing its DNA from every cell in the body","To make females haploid","To increase X gene expression","To allow recombination"],"e":"Every female has two Xs but one is <b>inactivated</b> (packed away “in the loft”), otherwise females would make <b>double the X gene products</b> compared with males. A female with three Xs has two inactivated. (The recording said “euchromatin” – likely a slip for heterochromatin.)","w":["It is packed away, not destroyed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"X inactivation balances dosage.","rn":[["","Why is one X inactivated in females?","Why is one X inactivated in females?","Else double X products; Compared to males"]]},{"id":24032,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which is the best definition of DNA damage/mutation?","o":["Any change in DNA sequence, repaired or not","Only harmful, unrepaired changes in the germline that are passed to children","Only large deletions of chromosomes","Only changes caused by radiation","Only changes in mitochondria"],"e":"DNA damage is <b>mutation</b> – <b>any change in the DNA sequence</b>, germline or soma, repaired or not, a deviation from “normal”. Causes include <b>radiation</b> and <b>chemicals</b>, and replication errors (chance).","w":["Any change, germline or soma, repaired or not.","Small too.","Any cause.","Not."],"k":"Mutation = any DNA change.","rn":[["","What is DNA damage?","What is DNA damage?","Mutation; Any DNA sequence change"]]},{"id":24033,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"What distinguishes a mutation from a polymorphism?","o":["A mutation changes a codon; a change that doesn’t is a polymorphism","A polymorphism changes a codon; a change that doesn’t is a mutation","Both always change codons","Mutations occur only in introns","Polymorphisms always cause disease"],"e":"A <b>mutation</b> creates a <b>new codon</b> (three nucleotides = one amino acid) in coding areas. Changes that don't change a codon (e.g. <b>intergenic</b>) have no effect: <b>polymorphisms (SNPs)</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mutation vs polymorphism.","rn":[["","What are changes in the DNA that do not change a codon called?","What are changes in the DNA that do not change a codon called?","SNPs (single nucleotide polymorphisms)"]]},{"id":24034,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which statement about mutations is correct?","o":["Source of variation; random; good, neutral or bad; low rates","Source of variation; directed; always harmful; low rates","Source of variation; random; always harmful; high rates","Never random and never advantageous","Never contribute to evolution"],"e":"Mutations: changes in DNA; <b>source of all genetic variation</b>; <b>random</b>; <b>harmful, neutral or advantageous</b>; rates <b>generally low</b>. All evolution is underpinned by advantageous mutations.","w":["Random.","Rates low.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Mutation features.","rn":[["","What effects can random mutations have?","What effects can random mutations have?","Harmful; Neutral; Advantageous"],["","What are mutations?","What are mutations?","Changes in DNA; Source of all genetic variation"],["","What is the source of all genetic variation?","What is the source of all genetic variation?","Mutations"]]},{"id":24035,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which pairing of mutation location and consequence is correct?","o":["Germline – heritable; somatic – ageing and disease","Germline – ageing and disease; somatic – heritable","Both always heritable","Somatic – passed to children","Germline – never passed on"],"e":"<b>Germline</b> mutations produce <b>heritable</b> changes; <b>somatic</b> mutations contribute to <b>ageing and disease</b>, e.g. uncontrolled growth (cancer).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Passed."],"k":"Germline heritable; somatic disease.","rn":[["","What do somatic mutations contribute to?","What do somatic mutations contribute to?","Ageing; Disease"],["","What do germline mutations produce?","What do germline mutations produce?","Heritable changes"]]},{"id":24036,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","f":"di28","s":"According to the distribution of mutation effects on fitness, most mutations are:","o":["Slightly deleterious, with a small beneficial tail","Strongly beneficial, with a small harmful tail","Always lethal","Exactly neutral","Equally split"],"e":"Curve peaks just below 1: most <b>slightly deleterious</b>; a small tail beneficial (>1); a spike at 0 (lethal/sterile). “Most mutations are well tolerated. Some are good, and a small number are quite bad.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Most slightly deleterious.","rn":[["","What is the fitness distribution of mutations?","What is the fitness distribution of mutations?","Most slightly deleterious; Some beneficial; Some cause lethality/sterility"]]},{"id":24037,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which description of cancer is correct in terms of the cell cycle?","o":["Unregulated division, e.g. a checkpoint mutation","Normal division in response to injury, which then overshoots and forms a mass","Only a replication error","A viral infection","Loss of all DNA"],"e":"Cancer is <b>unregulated proliferation</b> – “unstoppable division”. A mutation can interfere with a <b>checkpoint</b>, letting replication errors escape. What started division varies; the point is <b>why it doesn't stop</b>.","w":["He described it as unregulated division.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cancer = division that won't stop.","rn":[["","How can a mutation lead to cancer?","How can a mutation lead to cancer?","Tells cell keep dividing; Or disrupts a checkpoint"],["","If a mutation affects a checkpoint, what happens?","If a mutation affects a checkpoint, what happens?","Errors in replication escape; Damaged cell keeps replicating"]]},{"id":24038,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"By roughly how much does polymerase proofreading improve fidelity, and how many DNA repair genes exist?","o":["~100-fold; >130 repair genes","~2-fold; about 10 repair genes","~1 million-fold; 5 repair genes","~10-fold; no repair genes","No improvement; 1,000 genes"],"e":"Proofreading (3′-5′ exonuclease) improves fidelity by <b>~100</b>; <b>>130 repair genes</b> in the human genome.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Proofreading ~100×; >130 genes.","rn":[["","How many repair genes exist in the human genome?","How many repair genes exist in the human genome?",">130"]]},{"id":24039,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Name the repair system that removes only a single damaged base and replaces it.","e":"Base excision repair removes one damaged base; nucleotide excision repair removes ~30 nucleotides; mismatch repair corrects mispairs; recombination repair fixes double-strand breaks.","k":"Base excision repair.","a":["base excision repair","ber","base excision"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 excision repair types?","What are the 3 excision repair types?","Base excision; Nucleotide excision; Mismatch excision"],["","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","Base excision repair (BER); Nucleotide excision repair (NER); Mismatch repair (MMR)"],["","Base vs nucleotide excision repair: what is removed","Base vs nucleotide excision repair: what is removed","BER: just the base; NER: whole nucleotide"]]},{"id":24040,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Name the repair route that fixes double-stranded breaks and larger damaged areas.","e":"Recombination repair is related to meiotic recombination.","k":"Recombination repair.","a":["recombination repair","homologous recombination","homologous recombination repair","recombinational repair"],"rn":[["","What specific system repairs double stranded breaks?","What specific system repairs double stranded breaks?","Recombination repair"],["","Which repair can correct bigger areas of DNA?","Which repair can correct bigger areas of DNA?","Recombination repair"],["","The double strand break repair system is related to what?","The double strand break repair system is related to what?","The system that allows recombination"]]},{"id":24041,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Why are repair systems needed in addition to polymerase proofreading?","o":["To fix damage outside replication","To replace DNA polymerase, which has no proofreading activity of its own","To act only in gametes","Because proofreading does not exist","To cause useful mutations"],"e":"Besides proofreading during replication, repair systems fix mutations occurring <b>outside replication</b> or that <b>survived</b> replication.","w":["Polymerase does proofread; repair fixes other damage and missed errors.","Not.","Exists.","Not."],"k":"Repair beyond proofreading.","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of mistake are fixed by repair systems rather than proofreading?","What 2 kinds of mistake are fixed by repair systems rather than proofreading?","Mutations outside of replication; Mistakes the polymerase missed"]]},{"id":24042,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which enzyme activity is common to all the excision repair processes?","o":["Endonuclease – cutting from inside the DNA","Exonuclease – removing only end nucleotides","Ligase – joining fragments only","Helicase – unwinding only","Reverse transcriptase"],"e":"All excision processes involve <b>endonuclease</b> activity (cutting inside), compared with polymerase's <b>exonuclease</b> (end) activity.","w":["Polymerase.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Excision uses endonuclease.","rn":[["","Endonuclease activity","Endonuclease activity","Cutting out something; Inside the DNA"]]},{"id":24043,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"What distinguishes dominant from recessive inheritance?","o":["Dominant: one mutated copy; recessive: both copies","Dominant: both copies; recessive: one mutated copy","Dominant: only on X; recessive: only on autosomes","Both need two mutated copies","They are the same"],"e":"<b>Dominant</b>: one copy (maternal or paternal). <b>Recessive</b>: both copies. Mutations can be autosomal or sex-linked. Consider the inheritance pattern to determine the need for testing.","w":["Swapped.","Both.","Not.","Different."],"k":"Dominant 1 copy; recessive 2.","rn":[["","Dominant vs recessive: copies needed for disease","Dominant vs recessive: copies needed for disease","Dominant: one; Recessive: both"],["","Dominant inheritance","Dominant inheritance","Only one copy; Needs the mutation"]]},{"id":24044,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which pairing of disease, gene and inheritance is correct?","o":["DMD – X-linked recessive","DMD – autosomal dominant","SMA – X-linked dominant","XP – X-linked recessive","CF – mitochondrial"],"e":"<b>DMD</b> (DMD gene): <b>X-linked recessive</b>. <b>SMA</b> (SMA1): <b>autosomal recessive</b>. XP: autosomal recessive.","w":["X-linked.","AR.","AR.","AR."],"k":"DMD X-linked recessive; SMA AR.","rn":[["","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","X linked recessive"],["","What 2 pairs of terms describe disease-causing mutations?","What 2 pairs of terms describe disease-causing mutations?","Dominant vs recessive; Autosomal vs sex-linked"]]},{"id":24045,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which novel treatments can now correct single mutations?","o":["Gene therapy and CRISPR/Cas9","Antibiotics and antivirals","Chemotherapy","Vaccines","Surgery"],"e":"Novel treatments: <b>gene therapy, CRISPR/Cas9</b> – available healthcare or clinical trials.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gene therapy, CRISPR.","rn":[["","What 2 novel treatments can correct mutations?","What 2 novel treatments can correct mutations?","Gene therapy; CRISPR/Cas9"]]},{"id":24046,"t":"Inherited Diseases","f":"di34","s":"Two unaffected carrier parents have a child with XP. What is the inheritance pattern and the probability another child is affected?","o":["Autosomal recessive; 1 in 4","Autosomal recessive; 1 in 2","Autosomal dominant; 1 in 2","X-linked recessive; all sons","Mitochondrial; all children"],"e":"XP is <b>autosomal recessive</b>: both parents are carriers (one mutated copy each); the affected child inherited the mutated copy from <b>both</b>. Family tree: one of four children affected, two carriers, one unaffected → 25% risk per child.","w":["1 in 4.","AR.","Autosomal.","Not."],"k":"AR carriers → 25%.","rn":[["","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","Child with 2 mutated genes"]]},{"id":24047,"t":"Inherited Diseases","f":"di36","s":"What is the defect in xeroderma pigmentosum and its main consequences?","o":["Faulty UV repair (NER); sun sensitivity and many cancers","Faulty UV repair (BER); sun sensitivity and many cancers","Faulty chloride channels; mucus build-up","Trisomy 21","Unequal crossing over on chromosome 17"],"e":"<b>XP</b>: autosomal recessive mutation in the <b>UV repair mechanism</b> (NER). UV damage can't be repaired → <b>acute sun sensitivity</b>, <b>multiple cancers</b>; also hypo/hyper-pigmentation, mental retardation, progressive degeneration.","w":["NER.","CF.","Not.","CMT."],"k":"XP = NER defect.","rn":[["","What are the 3 consequences of UV damage in xeroderma pigmentosum?","What are the 3 consequences of UV damage in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Can't be repaired; Acute sun sensitivity; Multiple cancers"]]},{"id":24048,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","f":"di35","s":"About how long a stretch of DNA is excised in nucleotide excision repair?","o":["About 30 nucleotides","One base","146 bp","About 300 nucleotides","1.5 Mbp"],"e":"NER: proteins (XPC, TFIIH, XPA, RPA…) find damage, open DNA (helicase, ATP), cut out ~<b>30 nucleotides</b>, polymerase fills the gap. You don't need to learn the proteins.","w":["BER.","Nucleosome.","Not.","CMT."],"k":"NER excises ~30 nt.","rn":[["","How long is the DNA stretch removed in nucleotide excision repair (NER)?","How long is the DNA stretch removed in nucleotide excision repair (NER)?","About 30 nucleotides"]]},{"id":24049,"t":"Inherited Diseases","f":"di37","s":"What is the molecular defect in cystic fibrosis?","o":["CFTR Cl⁻ channels blocked or too few, causing mucus build-up","CFTR Na⁺ channels blocked or too few, causing mucus build-up","Defective DNA repair","An extra chromosome","Defective collagen"],"e":"<b>CFTR</b> is a <b>Cl⁻ channel</b> in epithelia; mutations give <b>blocked channels</b> or <b>few channels</b> → mucus build-up. <b>>450</b> known mutations; most common <b>ΔF508</b> (90% have at least one allele). Splicing errors covered in Gene Expression; more in HLB.","w":["Cl⁻.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CF = CFTR Cl⁻ channel.","rn":[["","What are the 2 forms CF mutations may take?","What are the 2 forms CF mutations may take?","Blocked channels; Few channels on surface"]]},{"id":24050,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"What proportion of CF patients carry at least one ΔF508 allele?","o":["90%","50%","10%","25%","100%"],"e":"<b>ΔF508</b>: <b>90%</b> of CF patients have at least one allele.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not all."],"k":"ΔF508 in 90%.","rn":[["","What % of CF patients have at least one ΔF508 allele?","What % of CF patients have at least one ΔF508 allele?","90%"]]},{"id":24051,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Why are mitochondrial diseases maternally inherited?","o":["The egg supplies the mitochondria; sperm only DNA","The sperm supplies the mitochondria; egg only DNA","Mitochondria lie on the X chromosome","They are autosomal recessive","Fathers pass no DNA"],"e":"At fertilisation the <b>egg carries the organelles</b>; the <b>sperm just contributes the DNA</b>. So mtDNA mutations are maternally inherited.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","They do."],"k":"mtDNA from egg.","rn":[["","Why are mitochondrial diseases maternally inherited?","Why are mitochondrial diseases maternally inherited?","The egg carries the organelles; Sperm only contributes DNA"]]},{"id":24052,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A woman in her 40s develops painless, subacute central vision loss due to an mtDNA mutation. Name the disease.","e":"Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) is a mitochondrial disease, maternally inherited.","k":"LHON.","a":["leber's hereditary optic neuropathy","leber hereditary optic neuropathy","lhon","lebers hereditary optic neuropathy"],"rn":[["","LHON","LHON","Leber's hereditary optic atrophy"]]},{"id":24053,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which mutation causes 80–90% of MERRF cases?","o":["A→G at mtDNA 8,344, disrupting mtDNA translation","A→G at mtDNA 8,344, disrupting nuclear gene transcription","ΔF508 in CFTR","Trisomy 13","PMP22 duplication"],"e":"<b>MERRF</b> (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibres): <b>80–90%</b> from <b>A→G at nucleotide 8,344</b>, defecting translation of all mtDNA-encoded genes. Covered further in NME.","w":["mtDNA genes.","CF.","Not.","CMT."],"k":"MERRF A→G 8344.","rn":[["","What does the MERRF mutation cause?","What does the MERRF mutation cause?","Translation defect; All mtDNA-encoded genes"]]},{"id":24054,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"How can a woman with an mtDNA mutation avoid passing it on?","o":["IVF with a donor egg: donor mitochondria, parents’ nuclear DNA","IVF with donor sperm: donor mitochondria, mother’s nuclear DNA","Gene therapy of the sperm","Antibiotics before conception","It cannot be avoided"],"e":"IVF with a <b>donor egg</b>: nuclear DNA from the mother, mitochondria from the donor; the media called it a “three-parent baby” but it isn't really.","w":["Egg carries them.","Not.","Not.","It can."],"k":"Donor egg avoids mtDNA disease.","rn":[["","How can mitochondrial disease be avoided in a child?","How can mitochondrial disease be avoided in a child?","IVF with a donor egg"],["","Is it really a three-parent baby?","Is it really a three-parent baby?","No, just a donor egg"]]},{"id":24055,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A couple's first child has Down syndrome. The mother is 42. Which mechanism and risk factor are most relevant?","o":["Non-disjunction (trisomy 21); maternal age","Non-disjunction (trisomy 18); maternal age","Unequal crossing over; paternal age","A CFTR mutation","X inactivation failure"],"e":"Down syndrome = trisomy 21 from non-disjunction; strongly linked to maternal (and paternal) age, rising steeply 30–40.","w":["21.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Trisomy 21 + maternal age.","rn":[["","What are trisomies 13, 18 and 21 strongly linked to?","What are trisomies 13, 18 and 21 strongly linked to?","Maternal AND paternal age"]]},{"id":24056,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"A mutation in a somatic skin cell from UV exposure is most likely to:","o":["Affect only that person (e.g. cancer)","Be inherited by all of their children, as the skin cells divide often","Cause trisomy in the skin","Alter mitochondrial inheritance","Have no possible effect"],"e":"Somatic mutations contribute to ageing and disease but are not heritable; germline mutations are heritable.","w":["Somatic mutations are not passed on.","Not.","Not.","Could."],"k":"Somatic: not heritable."},{"id":24057,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Why is a mutation in an intergenic region usually without effect?","o":["It changes no codon – a polymorphism","It changes a codon in a protein","It deletes a chromosome","It affects mitochondria","It always causes disease"],"e":"Changes in intergenic regions don't change codons – polymorphisms with no effect.","w":["Intergenic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Intergenic → no effect.","rn":[["","What are changes in intergenic regions called?","What are changes in intergenic regions called?","Polymorphisms"],["","What are changes in the DNA that do not change a codon called?","What are changes in the DNA that do not change a codon called?","SNPs (single nucleotide polymorphisms)"]]},{"id":24058,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which statement summarises the relationship between mitosis and meiosis phases?","o":["Same phases and spindles; meiosis has two divisions and recombination","Same phases and spindles; mitosis has two divisions and recombination","No shared phases; meiosis lacks a spindle","Mitosis involves recombination","Meiosis has one division"],"e":"Phases are the same for mitosis and meiosis, and microtubules build the spindle; meiosis has two divisions and recombination.","w":["Swapped.","Shared.","No.","Two."],"k":"Mitosis vs meiosis.","rn":[["","Which 4 phases are the same in mitosis and meiosis?","Which 4 phases are the same in mitosis and meiosis?","Prophase; Metaphase; Anaphase; Telophase"],["","Mitosis vs meiosis: number of cell divisions","Mitosis vs meiosis: number of cell divisions","Mitosis: one; Meiosis: two"],["","What 3 things does meiosis I involve?","What 3 things does meiosis I involve?","DNA replication; Recombination; Cell division"]]},{"id":24059,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A boy has Duchenne muscular dystrophy; his parents are unaffected. Through whom was it most likely inherited?","o":["His mother, a carrier of X-linked recessive DMD","His father, via the Y chromosome","Both parents equally (autosomal recessive)","His father, via mitochondria","It cannot be inherited"],"e":"DMD is <b>X-linked recessive</b>; boys have one X, from their mother.","w":["Y.","X-linked.","Maternal.","It can."],"k":"X-linked recessive via mother.","rn":[["","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","X linked recessive"]]},{"id":24061,"t":"Meiosis","s":"After DNA replication but before division, how many copies of each chromosome does a diploid cell have?","o":["Four (2 homologues × 2 chromatids)","Two (one maternal, one paternal homologue)","One","Eight","Three"],"e":"When DNA replicates each chromosome is copied, so there are <b>four copies</b> just before division (draw it down).","w":["Each homologue now has two chromatids, so four.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Four copies after replication.","rn":[["","Sister chromatids","Sister chromatids","2 copies of one chromosome; After replication"]]},{"id":24062,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Name a homologous pair of replicated chromosomes paired in prophase I.","e":"A tetrad (bivalent) has four chromatids.","k":"Tetrad.","a":["tetrad","bivalent","tetrads"],"rn":[["","How many chromatids in a tetrad?","How many chromatids in a tetrad?","Four; (2 pairs of sister chromatids)"],["","Tetrad","Tetrad","Homologous chromosomes lined up; Each a sister chromatid pair"],["","What forms in mid-prophase I?","What forms in mid-prophase I?","Pairs of homologs; Tetrads"]]},{"id":24063,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which division in meiosis reduces chromosome number from diploid to haploid?","o":["Meiosis I (homologues separate)","Meiosis II (sister chromatids separate)","Mitosis","S phase","Fertilisation"],"e":"Meiosis I separates homologous pairs (the tetrad), halving the chromosome sets; meiosis II separates sister chromatids.","w":["Not.","Not.","Doubles.","Restores."],"k":"Meiosis I reduces ploidy.","rn":[["","What is separated in meiosis I?","What is separated in meiosis I?","The 2 homologues; Of each chromosome"],["","If sister chromatids fail to separate in meiosis II?","If sister chromatids fail to separate in meiosis II?","One cell: 2 copies; Other: no copies"]]},{"id":24064,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which event restores the diploid number after meiosis?","e":"Fertilisation unites two haploid gametes (n = 23) to give 2n = 46.","k":"Fertilisation.","a":["fertilisation","fertilization","conception"],"rn":[["","In the life cycle, which process turns haploid gametes into a diploid zygote?","In the life cycle, which process turns haploid gametes into a diploid zygote?","Fertilisation"],["","Diplontic life cycle: last 2 stages?","Diplontic life cycle: last 2 stages?","Fertilisation; Zygote (2n)"]]},{"id":24065,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A gamete with an extra chromosome 18 fertilises a normal gamete. Name the resulting syndrome.","e":"Trisomy 13 = Patau; 18 = Edwards; 21 = Down.","k":"Edwards syndrome.","a":["edwards syndrome","edward's syndrome","edwards","trisomy 18","edward's"],"rn":[["","Trisomy 18 results in what?","Trisomy 18 results in what?","Edward's Syndrome"],["","Down syndrome vs Patau/Edward: survival","Down syndrome vs Patau/Edward: survival","Down: viable; Patau/Edward: lethal early"]]},{"id":24066,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A gamete missing chromosome X fertilises an X-bearing gamete. What karyotype results?","o":["XO – Turner syndrome","XXY – Klinefelter syndrome","XXX","XYY","46,XX"],"e":"Missing sex chromosome → XO (Turner), viable.","w":["Missing.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"XO = Turner.","rn":[["","What is the karyotype in Turner Syndrome?","What is the karyotype in Turner Syndrome?","XO female"]]},{"id":24067,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Why do most autosomal trisomies appear clinically as miscarriages?","o":["They are embryonic lethal","They are always repaired","They cause Turner syndrome","They do not occur","They are mitochondrial"],"e":"Trisomies can occur in all chromosomes, but most lead to <b>embryonic lethality</b>, appearing as miscarriage.","w":["Not.","Not.","They do.","Not."],"k":"Most trisomies lethal.","rn":[["","What do trisomies of other chromosomes lead to?","What do trisomies of other chromosomes lead to?","Embryonic lethality"]]},{"id":24068,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"What causes DNA changes?","o":["Outside exposures and chance replication errors","Diet alone, through chemicals that bind directly to the DNA bases","Age alone","Viruses alone","Nothing"],"e":"Mutations come from <b>outside exposure</b> (radiation, chemicals) or <b>replication problems</b> (chance).","w":["Both outside exposures and chance errors.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Radiation, chemicals, errors.","rn":[["","Apart from exposures, what causes mutations?","Apart from exposures, what causes mutations?","DNA replication problems; (Chance events)"]]},{"id":24069,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which explains why advantageous mutations matter?","o":["Evolution rests on them","They cause most genetic disease","They do not exist in humans","They prevent variation","They occur only in mitochondria"],"e":"Mutations can be advantageous; all evolution is underpinned by them.","w":["Most disease mutations are harmful, not advantageous.","Exist.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Advantageous mutations → evolution.","rn":[["","What underpins evolution?","What underpins evolution?","Advantageous mutations"]]},{"id":24070,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Name the repair system defective in xeroderma pigmentosum.","e":"XP: faulty nucleotide excision repair of UV damage (linked neighbouring bases), causing sun sensitivity and many cancers.","k":"Nucleotide excision repair.","a":["nucleotide excision repair","ner","nucleotide excision"],"rn":[["","Which repair pathway is faulty in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Which repair pathway is faulty in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Nucleotide excision repair"],["","What are the 3 excision repair types?","What are the 3 excision repair types?","Base excision; Nucleotide excision; Mismatch excision"],["","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","Base excision repair (BER); Nucleotide excision repair (NER); Mismatch repair (MMR)"]]},{"id":24071,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"UV radiation damages DNA by which change shown?","o":["Neighbouring bases become linked","Hydrogen bonds between the two strands are all broken, separating the helix","Whole chromosomes are lost","Histones dissolve","Telomeres lengthen"],"e":"UV photon: neighbouring bases become linked, distorting the helix (before vs after).","w":["UV links adjacent bases, distorting the helix.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"UV links adjacent bases."},{"id":24072,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which inheritance statement about the four children in the XP family tree is correct?","o":["One affected, two carriers, one normal","Two affected sons, one carrier daughter and one child with two normal copies","All four carriers","All four affected","None affected"],"e":"Family tree: unaffected daughter (two normal), unaffected carrier son, unaffected carrier daughter, affected son.","w":["Only one child is affected.","Not.","Not.","One."],"k":"AR pedigree outcome.","rn":[["","What does a carrier have?","What does a carrier have?","One mutated gene; One normal gene"]]},{"id":24073,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A disease needs a faulty gene from both parents. Name this mode of inheritance.","e":"Recessive: both copies mutated; dominant: one copy suffices.","k":"Recessive.","a":["recessive","autosomal recessive"],"rn":[["","Dominant vs recessive: copies needed for disease","Dominant vs recessive: copies needed for disease","Dominant: one; Recessive: both"],["","Which is more rare: dominant or recessive inheritance?","Which is more rare: dominant or recessive inheritance?","Recessive"],["","What does dominant vs recessive define?","What does dominant vs recessive define?","How a mutation is inherited"]]},{"id":24074,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which statement about single-mutation diseases is correct?","o":["Most are described; combinations also matter","No single-mutation diseases have been fully described yet, so combinations are studied","All diseases are single-mutation","Single mutations never cause disease","Only mitochondrial ones"],"e":"Most, if not all, single-mutation diseases have been described; diseases can also be caused by combinations of mutations.","w":["Most single-mutation diseases are described.","Combinations.","They do.","Not."],"k":"Single-mutation diseases described."},{"id":24075,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which explains why a female with three X chromosomes is often asymptomatic?","o":["Two of her X chromosomes are inactivated","All three X chromosomes stay active","She lacks X genes","The Y compensates","Mitochondria compensate"],"e":"A female with three Xs has <b>two inactivated</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","No Y.","Not."],"k":"Extra X inactivated.","rn":[["","In an XXX female, how many X chromosomes are inactivated?","In an XXX female, how many X chromosomes are inactivated?","Two"]]},{"id":24076,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Why might Turner syndrome be diagnosed only at puberty?","o":["Sex-chromosome genes matter more at puberty","It develops for the first time at puberty","It is caused by puberty","Karyotypes change at puberty","It is infectious"],"e":"Symptoms are often diagnosed later, at <b>puberty</b>, when sex-chromosome genes come more into play.","w":["Present from conception.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Turner diagnosed at puberty.","rn":[["","Why are sex chromosome conditions often diagnosed at puberty?","Why are sex chromosome conditions often diagnosed at puberty?","Sex chromosome genes; Come more into play"]]},{"id":24077,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which pairing of repair route and description is correct?","o":["Mismatch repair – corrects a mismatch","Proofreading – repairs double-strand breaks after replication has finished","BER – removes whole nucleotides","NER – removes only bases","Direct reversal – cuts large stretches"],"e":"MMR corrects mismatches distorting structure; proofreading during replication; BER bases; NER nucleotides; direct reversal in place.","w":["Breaks use recombination repair.","Bases.","Nucleotides.","Not."],"k":"Repair route matching.","rn":[["","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","Base excision repair (BER); Nucleotide excision repair (NER); Mismatch repair (MMR)"],["","What does mismatch excision repair look at?","What does mismatch excision repair look at?","The mismatch; Something changing DNA structure"],["","What are the 3 excision repair types?","What are the 3 excision repair types?","Base excision; Nucleotide excision; Mismatch excision"]]},{"id":24078,"t":"Meiosis","s":"What is the significance of the Harlequin (FPG) staining image?","o":["It shows exchange between chromatids","It diagnoses cystic fibrosis by staining the mutated chromosome","It shows mitochondria","It measures age","It must be memorised"],"e":"Harlequin staining visually demonstrates exchange between chromosomes; you don't need to know the technique.","w":["It shows recombination/exchange.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Harlequin shows exchange.","rn":[["","What staining visually demonstrates exchange of genetic material between chromosomes?","What staining visually demonstrates exchange of genetic material between chromosomes?","Harlequin staining"],["","Image card: What type of staining is this? What does it show?","What type of staining is this? What does it show?","Harlequin staining (FPG); Exchange of genetic material; Between chromosomes"]]},{"id":24079,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which happens in metaphase I?","o":["Homologous pairs align on the plate","Sister chromatids align on the plate and separate","Nuclear membranes re-form","DNA replicates","Chromosomes decondense"],"e":"<b>Metaphase I</b>: chromosomes aligned on the equatorial plate, caught by the spindle (microtubules).","w":["Metaphase II/anaphase.","Telophase.","S.","Telophase."],"k":"Metaphase I alignment.","rn":[["","What happens in metaphase I?","What happens in metaphase I?","Chromosomes aligned at equatorial plate; Caught by spindle apparatus"]]},{"id":24080,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which statement about telophase II is correct?","o":["Nuclei re-form; cytokinesis gives four haploid gametes","Nuclei re-form; cytokinesis gives two diploid cells","Recombination occurs","Homologues pair","DNA replicates"],"e":"<b>Telophase II</b>: nuclear membrane forms, chromosomes decondense, cytokinesis → <b>four haploid</b> gametes.","w":["Four haploid.","Prophase I.","Prophase I.","S."],"k":"Telophase II outcome.","rn":[["","What 3 things happen in telophase II?","What 3 things happen in telophase II?","Nuclear membrane forms; Chromosomes decondense; Cell splits (cytokinesis)"]]},{"id":24081,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which is the main difference between prophase I and prophase II?","o":["Prophase I pairs and recombines; prophase II only condenses","Prophase II pairs and recombines; prophase I only condenses","They are identical","Prophase I separates sisters","Prophase II replicates DNA"],"e":"Prophase I: condense, pair (tetrad), recombine. Prophase II: chromosomes condense.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Anaphase II.","Not."],"k":"Prophase I vs II.","rn":[["","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","Condense; Pair up with their homologue"],["","What forms in mid-prophase I?","What forms in mid-prophase I?","Pairs of homologs; Tetrads"]]},{"id":24082,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Name the neuropathy caused by unequal crossing over that duplicates PMP22.","e":"Charcot–Marie–Tooth type 1A results from PMP22 duplication by unequal crossing over.","k":"Charcot–Marie–Tooth 1A.","a":["charcot-marie-tooth","charcot marie tooth","cmt","cmt1a","charcot-marie-tooth 1a","charcot-marie-tooth type 1a"],"rn":[["","CMT1A","CMT1A","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A"]]},{"id":24083,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A deletion of PMP22 (instead of duplication) results in:","o":["Neuropathy","No effect","Down syndrome","Cystic fibrosis","XP"],"e":"The corresponding <b>deletion of PMP22</b> also results in <b>neuropathy</b>.","w":["Effect.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PMP22 deletion → neuropathy.","rn":[["","What does deletion of PMP22 cause?","What does deletion of PMP22 cause?","Neuropathy"]]},{"id":24084,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which explains why a meiosis II non-disjunction leaves some gametes normal?","o":["Only one meiosis II cell is affected","Both meiosis II cells are always affected, but two gametes are rescued","Meiosis II does not separate anything","It produces diploid cells only","Recombination corrects it"],"e":"In meiosis II ND only one cell is affected, so two gametes are normal and two abnormal (one extra, one missing).","w":["The other cell divides normally.","It does.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Meiosis II ND: partial.","rn":[["","What are the 4 cells made by meiosis?","What are the 4 cells made by meiosis?","The gametes"],["","What does meiosis II involve?","What does meiosis II involve?","A further cell division"]]},{"id":24085,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which statement about the Y chromosome is correct?","o":["It has few genes, so XYY is tolerated","It has more genes than X, so XYY is lethal","It is inactivated in males","It carries mitochondrial genes","It is absent in males"],"e":"An extra Y is tolerated because the <b>Y contains a small number of genes</b>.","w":["Few.","Not.","Not.","Present."],"k":"Y has few genes.","rn":[["","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Why is an extra Y chromosome tolerated?","Y has few genes"]]},{"id":24086,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"A drug damages DNA in germ cells of a man. What is the potential consequence?","o":["Germline mutations passed to offspring","Only skin cancer in the man himself, as germ cells cannot be damaged","No effect on his children","Immediate trisomy in the man","Mitochondrial disease in children"],"e":"Germline mutations can be passed on to the next generation.","w":["Germ-cell damage can pass to children.","Germline.","Not.","Maternal."],"k":"Germline damage heritable.","rn":[["","Germline mutation in gametes: what happens?","Germline mutation in gametes: what happens?","Passed on; To the next generation"]]},{"id":24087,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which statement about the inheritance pattern and genetic testing is correct?","o":["Use the pattern to decide on testing","Test every relative for every gene, whatever the inheritance pattern","Never test relatives","Inheritance pattern is irrelevant to testing","Only test mitochondria"],"e":"“Consider the inheritance pattern to determine the need for testing.” NHS genetic testing options exist.","w":["The pattern guides who to test.","Not.","Relevant.","Not."],"k":"Pattern guides testing."},{"id":24088,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"What is a codon?","o":["Three nucleotides for one amino acid","One nucleotide for one amino acid","A whole gene","A chromosome","A nucleosome"],"e":"A <b>codon</b> is <b>three nucleotides</b> corresponding to an <b>amino acid</b>.","w":["Three.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Codon = 3 nt.","rn":[["","Codon","Codon","Three nucleotides; Correspond to one amino acid"]]},{"id":24092,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which condition is also caused by a single mutation?","o":["It is also due to a single mutation","It is a trisomy","It is mitochondrial","It is X-linked","It is from unequal crossing over"],"e":"Examples of single-mutation diseases: DMD, SMA and <b>sickle cell anaemia</b> (also due to a single mutation).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sickle cell: single mutation.","rn":[["","Name 3 diseases caused by single mutations.","Name 3 diseases caused by single mutations.","Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy; Spinal Muscular Atrophy; Sickle cell anaemia"]]},{"id":24093,"t":"Meiosis","s":"What does “somatic cells are identical” mean?","o":["They contain the same genetic material","They look the same under a microscope","They do the same job","They are gametes","They are haploid"],"e":"“Identical” somatic cells means they contain the <b>same genetic material</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Diploid."],"k":"Same genetic material.","rn":[["","Recombinant chromatids","Recombinant chromatids","Contain genetic material; From different homologs"]]},{"id":24094,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which chromosomes are autosomes?","o":["All chromosomes except X and Y","Only X and Y","Only chromosome 1","Mitochondrial chromosomes","Chromosomes 13, 18 and 21"],"e":"All chromosomes are autosomes apart from the <b>sex chromosomes</b> (X, Y).","w":["Sex.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Autosomes = non-sex.","rn":[["","Autosomes","Autosomes","All chromosomes except; Sex chromosomes"]]},{"id":24095,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A fetus has monosomy of an autosome such as chromosome 7. What outcome is most likely?","o":["Embryonic lethality – early miscarriage","Survival with mild symptoms, like XXX","Survival with Patau’s syndrome","Survival with Turner syndrome","Normal development"],"e":"Trisomy or monosomy is usually fatal, except for chromosomes 13, 18, 21 or the sex chromosomes. Other aneuploidies lead to embryonic lethality and appear as early miscarriage (about 50% of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities).","w":["XXX is a sex chromosome aneuploidy.","Patau’s is trisomy 13.","Turner is monosomy X.","Autosomal monosomy is usually lethal."],"k":"Review resources.","rn":[["","What do trisomies of other chromosomes lead to?","What do trisomies of other chromosomes lead to?","Embryonic lethality"]]},{"id":24096,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which statement about sister chromatids is correct?","o":["Identical copies after replication, held until anaphase II","Maternal and paternal versions separated in anaphase I","Copies found only in gametes","Different genes on one chromosome","Homologues that pair in prophase I"],"e":"Sister chromatids: identical copies after replication; stay together in anaphase I, separate in anaphase II.","w":["Homologues.","Not.","Not.","Homologues."],"k":"Sister chromatids.","rn":[["","Sister chromatids","Sister chromatids","2 copies of one chromosome; After replication"]]},{"id":24097,"t":"Meiosis","s":"What happens during cohesion in recombination?","o":["Sister chromatids stay held together","Sister chromatids separate early so a chiasma can form between them","DNA replicates","Mitochondria divide","X is inactivated"],"e":"Because sister chromatids are held together (cohesion), a chiasma forms after break and rejoin.","w":["Cohesion holds them together.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cohesion → chiasma.","rn":[["","In anaphase I, what stays together?","In anaphase I, what stays together?","The sister chromatids"]]},{"id":24098,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which inheritance pattern is shown by a disease affecting both sexes equally, transmitted when both parents are carriers?","o":["Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial","Y-linked"],"e":"Autosomal (both sexes), recessive (both copies needed, carrier parents) – e.g. XP, SMA.","w":["Carriers unaffected.","Both sexes.","Maternal.","Males."],"k":"AR pattern.","rn":[["","What is the inheritance of xeroderma pigmentosum?","What is the inheritance of xeroderma pigmentosum?","Autosomal recessive"],["","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","Child with 2 mutated genes"],["","What is the inheritance of Spinal Muscular Atrophy?","What is the inheritance of Spinal Muscular Atrophy?","Autosomal recessive"]]},{"id":24099,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Why are mitochondrial mutations able to cause significant pathology?","o":["Mitochondria are vital for energy","Mitochondria make all proteins","Mitochondria store nuclear DNA","Mitochondria control meiosis","Mitochondria come from fathers"],"e":"Because mitochondria are very important for <b>energy production</b>, mutations can cause significant pathology.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Mothers."],"k":"mtDNA → energy failure.","rn":[["","Why can mitochondrial mutations be significant?","Why can mitochondrial mutations be significant?","Mitochondria matter for; Energy production"]]},{"id":24100,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which statement about genetic diseases is correct?","o":["Different mutations, with predictable patterns","Random events whose inheritance patterns cannot be predicted from the genes affected","Never inherited","Only chromosomal","Untreatable"],"e":"Take-home: meiosis/recombination → diversity; multiple repair systems critical; genetic diseases from different mutations with <b>predictable</b> inheritance patterns from gene location and function.","w":["Patterns are predictable from the affected genes.","Can be.","Not only.","Some treatable."],"k":"Take-home message.","rn":[["","What do genetic diseases result from?","What do genetic diseases result from?","Different types of mutation"]]},{"id":24101,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"What links increasing parental age to meiotic errors?","o":["Meiosis is more error-prone with age","Older people have more chromosomes","Age prevents recombination","Age increases X inactivation","Age has no effect"],"e":"As we age, these processes are <b>more prone to going wrong</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","It does."],"k":"Age → errors.","rn":[["","Why are meiotic errors linked to parental age?","Why are meiotic errors linked to parental age?","Processes more error-prone; With age"]]},{"id":24102,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which statement about mutation rates is correct?","o":["Generally low relative to replication","Very high, matching the rate of replication errors before repair","Zero","Doubling each generation","Only in cancer"],"e":"Compared to how much DNA and replication there is, the rate is generally quite low.","w":["Rates are low.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Low mutation rates.","rn":[["","Are mutation rates high or low?","Are mutation rates high or low?","Generally low"],["","What are mutation rates like?","What are mutation rates like?","Generally low"]]},{"id":24103,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which is the best description of a monosomy?","o":["One copy instead of two","Three copies instead of two","Four copies","Normal diploid","An extra Y"],"e":"Monosomy: one copy; trisomy: three copies.","w":["Trisomy.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Monosomy = one copy.","rn":[["","Monosomy","Monosomy","Missing a copy; Of one chromosome"]]},{"id":24104,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which explains why somatic mutations are “better tolerated” than germline ones?","o":["They aren’t inherited, but can cause cancer","They are always repaired before the cell divides, so have no effect","They never affect function","They are in gametes","They are polymorphisms"],"e":"Somatic mutations are better tolerated but can still affect function and cause uncontrolled growth.","w":["They can cause cancer, but are not inherited.","They can.","Germline.","Not."],"k":"Somatic tolerance.","rn":[["","What can mutations in mitochondrial genes cause?","What can mutations in mitochondrial genes cause?","A range of maternally-inherited diseases"]]},{"id":24105,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which features are typical of Down syndrome?","o":["Facial features, slow growth, cognitive impairment","XO karyotype, short stature and widely spaced nipples from birth","Painless vision loss","Sun sensitivity","Mucus build-up"],"e":"Down syndrome: distinct facial features, generally slow growth, some degree of cognitive impairment.","w":["That is Turner syndrome.","LHON.","XP.","CF."],"k":"Down features.","rn":[["","What are the 3 features of trisomy 21?","What are the 3 features of trisomy 21?","Distinct facial features; Generally slow growth rate; Some cognitive impairment"]]},{"id":24107,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"Which statement about recombination repair and recombination is correct?","o":["Break repair is related to meiotic recombination","The two systems are entirely unrelated, as break repair only happens in gametes","It works only in gametes","It uses polymerase proofreading","It removes single bases"],"e":"A specific system repairs double-stranded breaks; related to the recombination system.","w":["They are related.","All cells.","Not.","BER."],"k":"DSB repair related to recombination.","rn":[["","The double strand break repair system is related to what?","The double strand break repair system is related to what?","The system that allows recombination"],["","What specific system repairs double stranded breaks?","What specific system repairs double stranded breaks?","Recombination repair"]]},{"id":24108,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which of these is <b>not</b> an additional symptom of XP listed?","o":["Mucus build-up in lungs","Hypo- and hyper-pigmentation","Mental retardation","Progressive degeneration","Acute sun sensitivity"],"e":"XP additional symptoms: hypo/hyper-pigmentation, mental retardation, progressive degeneration; plus sun sensitivity and multiple cancers. Mucus = CF.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"XP symptoms."},{"id":24109,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which statement about the number of CF mutations is correct?","o":["Over 450","Only one","Exactly 90","About 7,000","None"],"e":"<b>>450</b> known CF mutations; ΔF508 most common.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":">450 CF mutations.","rn":[["","How many known CF mutations are there?","How many known CF mutations are there?","Over 450"]]},{"id":24111,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"Which explains why a person with XP is at risk of multiple cancers?","o":["They can’t repair UV damage","They have extra chromosomes that make cells divide uncontrollably in sunlight","They lack mitochondria","Their chloride channels are blocked","X inactivation fails"],"e":"Two faulty NER genes → cannot repair DNA → more susceptible to mutations and pathologies.","w":["XP is a repair defect.","Not.","CF.","Not."],"k":"Unrepaired UV damage → cancer.","rn":[["","Image card: Mutations in this repair pathway cause which disease? What damage does it repair?","Mutations in this repair pathway cause which disease? What damage does it repair?","Xeroderma pigmentosum; UV damage"],["","What are the 3 consequences of UV damage in xeroderma pigmentosum?","What are the 3 consequences of UV damage in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Can't be repaired; Acute sun sensitivity; Multiple cancers"],["","Is all DNA damage repaired?","Is all DNA damage repaired?","No; Repaired or not"]]},{"id":24113,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Which explanation for why recombination errors cause gene loss is correct?","o":["One gives more than it takes back","Recombination exchanges no material at all, so genes are simply deleted","Polymerase destroys genes","X inactivation removes genes","Mitochondria absorb genes"],"e":"The amount exchanged may not be equal – one chromosome is “shortchanged”.","w":["Unequal exchange explains the loss.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Unequal exchange.","rn":[["","What happens when one chromosome gives more than it takes?","What happens when one chromosome gives more than it takes?","It loses genetic material"]]},{"id":24114,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which pair are written as gamete karyotypes?","o":["22X and 22Y","44XX and 44XY","46XX and 46XY","23XX and 23YY","45X and 47XXY"],"e":"Gametes: <b>22X</b> or <b>22Y</b>; somatic: 44XX/44XY.","w":["Somatic.","Full.","Not.","Aneuploid."],"k":"22X/22Y gametes.","rn":[["","How is a gamete chromosome number written?","How is a gamete chromosome number written?","22X or 22Y"]]},{"id":24115,"t":"Meiosis","s":"Which statement summarises meiosis I telophase?","o":["Two cells, one homologue of each pair","Four haploid cells, each chromosome now a single chromatid","Two cells after sister chromatids separate","DNA replicates","Recombination occurs"],"e":"Telophase I: cell divides (2 cells); each chromosome still has two sister chromatids; meiosis II follows.","w":["That is telophase II.","Meiosis II.","Not.","Prophase I."],"k":"Telophase I.","rn":[["","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","What 2 things happen to chromosomes in prophase I?","Condense; Pair up with their homologue"],["","What does each cell contain after meiosis I?","What does each cell contain after meiosis I?","Maternal or paternal homologue; Now recombined"]]},{"id":24116,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A mother is a carrier of Duchenne muscular dystrophy (X-linked recessive); the father is unaffected. What is the percentage chance that a son is affected?","e":"Each son has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutated X from his mother.","k":"50%.","a":["50","50%","50 percent","half","1 in 2"],"rn":[["","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","X linked recessive"],["","What proportion of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities?","What proportion of early miscarriages are due to chromosomal abnormalities?","~50%"]]},{"id":24117,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"Non-disjunction occurs in meiosis II in one cell only. How many of the four gametes are abnormal?","o":["Two (one extra, one missing)","All four (two extra, two missing)","One","Three","None"],"e":"In meiosis II non-disjunction only the cell where sister chromatids failed to separate is affected: it gives one gamete with an extra chromosome and one missing it. The other cell divides normally, giving two normal gametes. In meiosis I non-disjunction, all four are abnormal.","w":["That is meiosis I non-disjunction.","Two are affected.","Two.","Some."],"k":"Kucab 2019.","rn":[["","Non-disjunction in meiosis II: what gametes result?","Non-disjunction in meiosis II: what gametes result?","2 normal; 1 with an extra chromosome; 1 missing a chromosome"],["","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","Non-disjunction in meiosis I: what gametes result?","2 with an extra chromosome; 2 missing a chromosome"]]},{"id":24118,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A couple asks whether their child could inherit the father's mitochondrial disease. What is the answer?","o":["Unlikely – mitochondria come from the egg","Certain – mitochondria come from the sperm","50% – like autosomal dominant","Only daughters","Only sons"],"e":"Mitochondrial diseases are maternally inherited.","w":["Maternal.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Paternal mtDNA not passed."},{"id":24120,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A carrier of xeroderma pigmentosum has children with a partner who has two normal copies. What are the chances for each child?","o":["0% affected; 50% carriers","25% affected; 50% carriers","50% affected; 50% carriers","0% affected; 25% carriers","100% carriers"],"e":"XP is autosomal recessive: an affected child needs two mutated copies. With one carrier parent and one non-carrier, no child can inherit two mutated copies (0% affected), but each child has a 50% chance of being a carrier.","w":["Needs two carrier parents.","Recessive.","Half.","Half inherit the mutated copy."],"k":"Lecturer contact.","rn":[["","What does a carrier have?","What does a carrier have?","One mutated gene; One normal gene"],["","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","In autosomal recessive inheritance, who is affected?","Child with 2 mutated genes"]]},{"id":24121,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A 44-year-old woman and her 50-year-old partner have a baby with distinct facial features, slow growth and some cognitive impairment. A karyotype shows three copies of chromosome 21. The couple ask how this happened and whether their ages played a part.<br><br>What is the mechanism, and how is parental age involved?","o":["Non-disjunction in meiosis giving trisomy 21; linked to maternal and paternal age","Unequal crossing over giving a gene duplication; not linked to age","A somatic mutation after birth; linked only to the baby's age","X inactivation failure; linked to paternal age only","Non-disjunction giving monosomy 21, which is usually fatal and is linked only to the mother's age"],"e":"Step 1: non-disjunction in meiosis (chromosomes failing to separate) gives trisomy or monosomy, and trisomy 21 causes Down syndrome. Step 2: trisomies 21, 13 and 18 are all strongly linked to maternal and paternal age.","w":["Crossing-over errors cause duplications, not an extra whole chromosome.","The extra chromosome is present from conception, not a later somatic change.","X inactivation concerns sex chromosomes.","The baby has three copies (trisomy), and age of both parents matters."],"k":"Non-disjunction → trisomy 21; linked to maternal and paternal age.","rn":[["","What are trisomies 13, 18 and 21 strongly linked to?","What are trisomies 13, 18 and 21 strongly linked to?","Maternal AND paternal age"]]},{"id":24122,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A 16-year-old girl is referred because she has not started her periods. She is short, has a webbed neck and a heart defect, and scans show underdeveloped ovaries. A tall 25-year-old man is investigated for infertility and found to have an extra X chromosome.<br><br>Which karyotypes are these, and why are such conditions better tolerated than autosomal ones?","o":["Girl: Turner (XO); man: Klinefelter (XXY); X chromosome inactivation","Girl: Klinefelter (XXY); man: Turner (XO); X inactivation","Girl: triple X; man: XYY; Y inactivation","Girl: Turner (XO); man: Klinefelter (XXY); because the Y chromosome is inactivated in every cell","Girl: trisomy 18; man: trisomy 13; both are autosomal"],"e":"Step 1: Turner syndrome is an XO female with reduced height, underdeveloped ovaries and possible neck webbing and heart and kidney defects; Klinefelter syndrome is an XXY male with reduced fertility. Step 2: sex chromosome non-disjunction is well tolerated compared with autosomal non-disjunction, linked to X chromosome inactivation, and is often diagnosed at puberty or through fertility issues.","w":["The two syndromes are swapped.","Triple X and XYY are often not identified from symptoms.","It is X inactivation, not Y inactivation.","These are sex chromosome conditions, not autosomal trisomies."],"k":"Turner XO, Klinefelter XXY; tolerated due to X inactivation.","rn":[["","What are the 4 sex chromosome conditions?","What are the 4 sex chromosome conditions?","Turner (XO); Klinefelter (XXY); XXX females; XYY males"]]},{"id":24123,"t":"Meiosis","s":"A researcher follows a single germline cell through meiosis. Before the first division, the DNA is copied and homologous chromosomes pair to form tetrads and exchange segments. In the first division, the paired homologues separate but sister chromatids stay together. In the second division, there is no DNA copying.<br><br>What happens in meiosis II, and what is the final outcome?","o":["Sister chromatids separate; four haploid gametes","Homologues pair again; two diploid cells","DNA is replicated again; four diploid cells","Crossing over occurs again; two haploid gametes","Sister chromatids stay together; four identical diploid somatic cells, as in mitosis"],"e":"Step 1: meiosis I involves DNA replication, recombination (homologues paired as a tetrad crossing over at the chiasma) and separation of the tetrad, with sister chromatids staying together. Step 2: meiosis II has no DNA replication and separates the sister chromatids, giving four haploid (n) gametes from one diploid (2n) cell.","w":["Pairing of homologues happens in meiosis I.","There is no DNA replication before meiosis II.","Crossing over occurs in prophase I, and four cells result.","Sister chromatids separate in meiosis II, giving haploid gametes."],"k":"Meiosis II: no replication, sister chromatids separate → 4 haploid gametes.","rn":[["","If sister chromatids fail to separate in meiosis II?","If sister chromatids fail to separate in meiosis II?","One cell: 2 copies; Other: no copies"],["","In anaphase I, what stays together?","In anaphase I, what stays together?","The sister chromatids"],["","How many chromatids in a tetrad?","How many chromatids in a tetrad?","Four; (2 pairs of sister chromatids)"]]},{"id":24124,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A 6-year-old child has severe sunburn after minimal sun exposure, patches of both darker and lighter skin, and has already had several skin cancers removed. Both parents are healthy, but they are first cousins. Genetic testing shows a defect in the system that repairs UV damage by removing a damaged nucleotide and adding the correct one.<br><br>What is the diagnosis, its inheritance, and which repair system is faulty?","o":["Xeroderma pigmentosum; autosomal recessive; nucleotide excision repair","Cystic fibrosis; autosomal recessive; mismatch repair","Duchenne muscular dystrophy; X-linked recessive; base excision repair","Xeroderma pigmentosum; autosomal dominant; proofreading","MERRF; maternal inheritance; double-strand break recombination repair"],"e":"Step 1: xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutation in components of the UV repair mechanism, with sun sensitivity, hypo- and hyper-pigmentation and multiple cancers; healthy carrier parents fit recessive inheritance. Step 2: the faulty system is nucleotide excision repair (NER), which removes a damaged nucleotide and adds the correct one.","w":["CF is a Cl⁻ channel disease, not UV repair.","DMD is a muscle disease, not UV repair.","XP is recessive and involves NER, not proofreading.","MERRF is a mitochondrial translation defect."],"k":"XP: autosomal recessive NER defect → UV sensitivity, cancers.","rn":[["","Which repair pathway is faulty in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Which repair pathway is faulty in xeroderma pigmentosum?","Nucleotide excision repair"],["","Xeroderma pigmentosum is an example of what?","Xeroderma pigmentosum is an example of what?","Faulty DNA repair causing disease; Autosomal recessive disorder"],["","What is the inheritance of xeroderma pigmentosum?","What is the inheritance of xeroderma pigmentosum?","Autosomal recessive"]]},{"id":24125,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A 2-year-old child has repeated chest infections and thick secretions. Genetic testing shows a mutation in a protein that acts as a chloride channel in epithelial tissues; in her case, the protein is made but very few channels reach the cell surface. Her mutation is the most common one.<br><br>Which protein and mutation are most likely involved?","o":["CFTR; ΔF508, found on at least one allele in 90% of patients","CFTR; one of 450 rare mutations, none common","Dystrophin; a DMD gene deletion","SMA1; a mutation causing loss of motor neurons and weakness of the muscles","PMP22; a duplication from unequal crossing over"],"e":"Step 1: the CFTR protein is a Cl⁻ channel expressed in a number of epithelial tissues, and CF mutations can cause blocked channels or few channels on the membrane surface. Step 2: there are over 450 known CF mutations, but the most common is ΔF508; 90% of CF patients have at least one ΔF508 allele.","w":["ΔF508 is very common among CF patients.","DMD is a muscle disease gene.","SMA1 causes spinal muscular atrophy.","PMP22 duplication relates to CMT1A."],"k":"CF: CFTR Cl⁻ channel; ΔF508 in 90%.","rn":[["","What % of CF patients have at least one ΔF508 allele?","What % of CF patients have at least one ΔF508 allele?","90%"]]},{"id":24126,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A 5-year-old boy has progressive muscle weakness and is diagnosed with Duchenne muscular dystrophy. His mother and sister are healthy, and his maternal uncle had the same condition. His parents ask why only the males in the family seem affected.<br><br>What is the inheritance pattern, and why are mainly males affected?","o":["X-linked recessive; males have one X, so one faulty copy causes disease","Autosomal recessive; males need two copies","Autosomal dominant; one copy causes disease in anyone","Mitochondrial; passed only to sons from mothers","X-linked dominant; females are protected because they have two Y chromosomes"],"e":"Step 1: Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD gene) is X-linked recessive; recessive disease needs both gene copies affected, but males have only one X chromosome. Step 2: so a male with one faulty DMD copy is affected, while females with one faulty copy (like his mother) are usually healthy carriers, which explains the affected uncle and boy.","w":["DMD is X-linked, not autosomal.","DMD is recessive, and his mother is unaffected.","Mitochondrial disease passes to all children of affected mothers.","Females have two X chromosomes, not two Y."],"k":"DMD: X-linked recessive → mainly males affected.","rn":[["","Xeroderma pigmentosum is an example of what?","Xeroderma pigmentosum is an example of what?","Faulty DNA repair causing disease; Autosomal recessive disorder"],["","What 2 pairs of terms describe disease-causing mutations?","What 2 pairs of terms describe disease-causing mutations?","Dominant vs recessive; Autosomal vs sex-linked"],["","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","What is the inheritance of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?","X linked recessive"]]},{"id":24127,"t":"Inherited Diseases","s":"A 35-year-old man has a painless, rapid loss of central vision in both eyes over a few weeks. His mother's brother had the same problem, and his own children are unaffected. Testing shows a mutation in his mitochondrial DNA.<br><br>What is the likely diagnosis, and why are his children unaffected?","o":["LHON; mitochondrial disease is maternally inherited, so fathers do not pass it on","MERRF; it is X-linked recessive","LHON; it is autosomal dominant","Xeroderma pigmentosum; it is autosomal recessive","MERRF; it is passed from fathers to all of their children through the sperm mitochondria"],"e":"Step 1: Leber's hereditary optic atrophy (LHON) is a midlife, acute or subacute, painless central vision loss resulting from one of a range of mtDNA mutations. Step 2: mutations in mitochondrial genes cause maternally inherited diseases, so he inherited it through his mother's line but cannot pass it to his own children.","w":["MERRF causes myoclonic epilepsy, and mitochondrial disease is maternal.","LHON is mitochondrial, not autosomal dominant.","XP is a skin/UV repair disorder.","Mitochondrial disease is maternally, not paternally, inherited."],"k":"LHON: mtDNA, painless central vision loss, maternal inheritance.","rn":[["","What can mutations in mitochondrial genes cause?","What can mutations in mitochondrial genes cause?","A range of maternally-inherited diseases"],["","Why are mitochondrial diseases maternally inherited?","Why are mitochondrial diseases maternally inherited?","The egg carries the organelles; Sperm only contributes DNA"]]},{"id":24128,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"A 60-year-old woman who worked outdoors for years develops a skin cancer on her face caused by a mutation only in her skin cells. Her son, separately, inherited a disease-causing mutation from his father that is present in every cell of his body.<br><br>Which types of mutation are these, and what are their consequences?","o":["Hers: somatic, contributing to ageing and disease; his: germline, heritable","Hers: germline, heritable; his: somatic","Both somatic, as neither is in the gametes","Both germline, as both cause disease","Hers: germline, passed to all her children; his: somatic, limited to one organ"],"e":"Step 1: somatic mutations occur in body cells; they are better tolerated but contribute to ageing and disease, including uncontrolled growth (cancer). Step 2: germline mutations occur in gametes and produce heritable changes passed to the next generation, so they appear in every cell of the child.","w":["The two are swapped.","His mutation was inherited, so it came from the germline.","Her mutation is only in skin cells, so it is somatic.","Her mutation is somatic and is not passed on."],"k":"Somatic: ageing/cancer; germline: heritable.","rn":[["","What do somatic mutations contribute to?","What do somatic mutations contribute to?","Ageing; Disease"],["","What do germline mutations produce?","What do germline mutations produce?","Heritable changes"]]},{"id":24129,"t":"DNA Damage and Repair","s":"A researcher studies three types of DNA damage in cells. In case 1, a single damaged base is removed and replaced. In case 2, a mismatched base pair that distorts the DNA structure is removed and corrected. In case 3, both strands of DNA are broken.<br><br>Which repair systems deal with each case?","o":["1: base excision repair; 2: mismatch repair; 3: recombination repair","1: mismatch repair; 2: base excision repair; 3: proofreading","1: nucleotide excision repair; 2: direct reversal; 3: BER","1: recombination repair; 2: NER; 3: MMR","All three by DNA polymerase proofreading with its 3′–5′ exonuclease activity"],"e":"Step 1: base excision repair (BER) removes and replaces just the damaged base, and mismatch repair (MMR) removes and corrects a mismatch that changes the DNA structure. Step 2: a specific system related to recombination repairs double-stranded breaks.","w":["BER and MMR are swapped, and proofreading acts during replication.","A single base is BER, and double-strand breaks use recombination repair.","Recombination repair is for double-strand breaks.","Proofreading occurs during replication only."],"k":"BER: damaged base; MMR: mismatch; recombination: double-strand breaks.","rn":[["","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","What are the 3 excision repair systems?","Base excision repair (BER); Nucleotide excision repair (NER); Mismatch repair (MMR)"],["","What are the 3 excision repair types?","What are the 3 excision repair types?","Base excision; Nucleotide excision; Mismatch excision"]]},{"id":24130,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"A 20-year-old man has a hereditary nerve disorder caused by having an extra copy of a gene for a peripheral myelin protein. His genetic counsellor explains that the extra copy arose when homologous chromosomes paired and exchanged segments unevenly during meiosis in one of his parents.<br><br>Which error caused this, and what other changes can such errors cause?","o":["Unequal crossing over; gene loss or duplication, inversions and translocations","Non-disjunction; trisomy and monosomy only","A somatic point mutation; cancer only","X inactivation failure; Turner syndrome","Mismatch repair failure; only single base changes in the protein that make up the myelin sheath"],"e":"Step 1: during prophase I, homologues pair and cross over (recombination); if this is unequal, one chromosome can gain an extra copy of a gene, as in CMT1A with an extra PMP22 copy. Step 2: recombination errors can lead to gene duplications (or loss) and to inversions and translocations.","w":["Non-disjunction changes whole chromosome numbers.","The change arose in a parent's meiosis and was inherited.","X inactivation is unrelated to gene duplication.","The extra gene copy is a duplication, not a single base change."],"k":"Unequal crossing over → duplication/loss, inversions, translocations (CMT1A).","rn":[["","What can recombination errors lead to?","What can recombination errors lead to?","Gene duplications (or loss); Inversions; Translocations"]]},{"id":24501,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"This karyotype is from a baby with characteristic facial features and slow growth. Name the syndrome.","a":["down syndrome","down's syndrome","downs syndrome","trisomy 21","down"],"e":"There are three copies of chromosome 21: trisomy 21 (Down syndrome), usually from non-disjunction.","k":"Trisomy 21 = Down syndrome.","img":{"src":"24/o_trisomy21","cap":"Karyotype. Image: U.S. Department of Energy Human Genome Program, public domain, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Trisomy 21 results in what?","Trisomy 21 results in what?","Down Syndrome"]]},{"id":24502,"t":"Meiotic Errors","s":"For the karyotype shown, which mechanism and risk factor are most likely?","o":["Meiotic non-disjunction; increasing maternal age","Unequal crossing over; increasing paternal age","Mitotic non-disjunction in the embryo; maternal smoking","A mitochondrial mutation; maternal inheritance","A single-gene recessive mutation; consanguinity"],"w":["Unequal crossing over causes duplications/deletions (e.g. CMT1A).","Mitotic non-disjunction gives mosaicism (distinct cell lines).","Mitochondrial disease is not a chromosome count change.","A recessive mutation does not add a chromosome."],"e":"Trisomy 21 usually results from meiotic non-disjunction; incidence rises steeply with maternal age from the mid-30s.","k":"Non-disjunction, maternal age.","img":{"src":"24/o_trisomy21","cap":"Karyotype. Image: U.S. Department of Energy Human Genome Program, public domain, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","How does the incidence of chromosomal abnormality change with maternal age?","How does the incidence of chromosomal abnormality change with maternal age?","Increases"],["","What is the key point of the maternal age data?","What is the key point of the maternal age data?","Increasing incidence with maternal age; Precise numbers not important"]]}]);
